OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026", "title": "A counterexample to Ryser's covering conjecture", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "162", "family_title": "Counterexamples to Ryser’s covering conjecture", "abstract_html": "For every sufficiently large prime \\(s\\equiv2\\pmod3\\) and every sufficiently large odd integer <i>n</i>, with the threshold depending on <i>s</i>, we construct an intersecting \\((s^n+1)\\)-partite \\((s^n+1)\\)-uniform hypergraph with covering number \\(s^n+1\\). This disproves Ryser's covering conjecture in its intersecting case.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">A vertex cover of a finite hypergraph \\(H\\) is a set of vertices meeting every edge. Its covering number \\(\\tau(H)\\) is the minimum size of a vertex cover.</p><p class=\"li\">An \\(r\\)-partite \\(r\\)-uniform hypergraph has pairwise disjoint vertex parts \\(V_1,\\ldots,V_r\\), and every edge contains exactly one vertex from each part.</p><p class=\"li\">A nonempty hypergraph is intersecting if every two distinct edges meet.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exists \\(s_0\\) such that, for every prime \\(s\\ge s_0\\) satisfying \\(s\\equiv2\\pmod3\\), there is an integer \\(n_0(s)\\ge3\\) with the following property. For every odd integer \\(n\\ge n_0(s)\\), put \\(q=s^n\\). There is a finite intersecting \\((q+1)\\)-partite \\((q+1)\\)-uniform hypergraph \\(H\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\tau(H)=q+1.\n\\]\nIn particular, Ryser’s covering conjecture\n\\[\n\\tau(H)\\le(r-1)\\nu(H)\n\\]\nis false in its intersecting case, where \\(\\nu(H)=1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix any sufficiently large prime \\(s\\equiv2\\pmod3\\). Choose the configuration of Proposition 4, then take \\(q=s^n\\) sufficiently large with \\(n\\) odd so that Proposition 5 applies. Form \\(H\\) as in Proposition 6.</p><p>It remains to exclude a vertex cover \\(C\\) with \\(|C|\\le q\\). Ordinary vertices of \\(C\\), regarded as affine lines, cover \\(X\\), because special vertices belong to no ordinary edge. Proposition 5 gives a projective point \\(p\\) such that at least \\((1-\\rho)q\\) ordinary vertices of \\(C\\) are lines through \\(p\\).</p><p>Every undeleted member of this pencil is forced into \\(C\\):\n\\[\np\\in\\ell,\\qquad \\ell\\notin B\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad o_\\ell\\in C.\n\\]\nIndeed, if \\(o_\\ell\\) were missing, all other lines of \\(C\\) through \\(p\\) would cover at most one affine point of \\(\\ell\\). At most \\(\\rho q\\) vertices lie outside the large pencil subcollection; each ordinary line among them covers at most one point of \\(\\ell\\), and special vertices cover no ordinary edges. Thus\n\\[\n|X\\cap\\ell|\\le1+\\rho q,\n\\]\ncontradicting\n\\[\n|X\\cap\\ell|\\ge\\delta q/2&gt;1+\\rho q\n\\]\nfor large \\(q\\), because \\(\\rho=\\delta/10\\). At infinity the pencil lines are parallel, so the same upper bound holds without the possible central point.</p><p>Let \\(F_p\\) be the set of these forced ordinary vertices. Call \\(C\\setminus F_p\\) the additional vertices.</p><p>First suppose \\(p\\) is the point at infinity of direction \\(d\\). Put\n\\[\nI_d=\\{i:d\\in S_i\\},\\qquad z=|I_d|\\in\\{1,2\\}.\n\\]\nOnly these groups supply deletion lines of direction \\(d\\), each supplying \\(s-1\\). At most \\(z(s-1)\\) parallel lines are deleted, so\n\\[\n|C\\setminus F_p|\\le z(s-1).\n\\]\nNo forced vertex covers an exceptional edge of a group in \\(I_d\\), because those groups use special vertices in direction \\(d\\).</p><p>If \\(z=1\\), all positions of this group’s \\(f(Y)\\) are covered by at most \\(s-1\\) additional vertices. Proposition 7 includes their actions in at most \\(s-1\\) subfield lines, contradicting Proposition 4.</p><p>If \\(z=2\\), the groups are \\(1,*\\). For each \\(i\\in\\{1,*\\}\\), let \\(a_i\\) count additional vertices that cover a position of group \\(i\\) and are either special vertices of that group or ordinary vertices in its axis directions \\(A_i\\). Let \\(b_i'\\) count the other additional vertices covering a position of the group. Proposition 7 makes the former line resources and the latter singleton resources. Proposition 4 gives\n\\[\na_i+b_i'/2\\ge s\\qquad(i=1,*).\n\\]</p><p>Every additional vertex contributes at most one to the sum of these two weighted costs. A special vertex is confined to its tagged group. An ordinary axis-direction vertex counted in \\(a_i\\) is a line meeting \\(P_i\\) with direction in \\(A_i\\subseteq D_i\\); compatibility prevents it from meeting the other group. Any vertex of neither exclusive type contributes at most \\(1/2\\) to each group. Therefore\n\\[\n2s\\le a_1+b_1'/2+a_*+b_*'/2\n\\le|C\\setminus F_p|\\le2(s-1),\n\\]\na contradiction.</p><p>Now suppose \\(p\\) is finite, and put\n\\[\nh_p=|\\{\\ell\\in B:p\\in\\ell\\}|.\n\\]\nA finite pencil has \\(q+1\\) affine lines, so\n\\[\n|F_p|=q+1-h_p,\\qquad\n|C\\setminus F_p|\\le h_p-1.\n\\]\nIf \\(h_p=0\\), the forced vertices already exceed \\(q\\). Assume \\(h_p\\ge1\\).</p><p>Suppose first that \\(p\\in P_i\\), for the necessarily unique group \\(i\\). Every deletion line through \\(p\\) comes from that group: a line from another group would violate compatibility. There is at most one such line in each direction of \\(S_i\\), hence\n\\[\nh_p\\le s-1.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nw_p=M_i^{-1}(p-b_i)\\in\\mathbb F_s^2,\n\\]\nwhether or not \\(w_p\\in Y\\).</p><p>Every exceptional position of group \\(i\\) covered by \\(F_p\\) lies on one of the two coordinate lines through \\(f(w_p)\\). For a base point \\(y\\ne p\\), its join with \\(p\\) has direction in \\(S_i\\cup A_i\\). A direction in \\(S_i\\) cannot cover the edge by an ordinary vertex, so actual forced coverage must be along an axis. A position based at \\(p\\), if present, lies on both axes. The map \\(f\\) preserves those parallel classes.</p><p>The additional vertices can each be included in one subfield line by Proposition 7. We therefore obtain a cover of \\(f(Y)\\) by at most\n\\[\n2+(h_p-1)\\le s\n\\]\nlines containing the two nonparallel coordinate lines through \\(f(w_p)\\). They are valid resources whether or not their ordinary labels belong to \\(F_p\\). Removing duplicate replacement lines preserves these two distinct lines and only decreases the count. This contradicts Proposition 4.</p><p>Finally suppose\n\\[\np\\notin\\bigcup_{i\\in I}P_i.\n\\]\nEach group supplies at most one deletion line through \\(p\\). Otherwise two distinct such lines have different directions; undoing \\(b_i,M_i\\) gives two affine equations in distinct subfield directions. Their nonsingular linear system over \\(\\mathbb F_s\\) has intersection in \\(\\mathbb F_s^2\\), forcing \\(p\\in P_i\\), a contradiction. Deletion lines from distinct groups cannot coincide, by compatibility.</p><p>The \\(h_p\\) deletion lines through \\(p\\) thus come from \\(h_p\\) distinct groups. Choose five distinct points of \\(Y_i\\) on each contributing line, possible by Proposition 5. They give \\(5h_p\\) distinct exceptional edges by Proposition 6. None is covered by a forced ordinary vertex: its unique join to \\(p\\) is its contributing deletion line, whose direction is altered for that group.</p><p>Every additional vertex covers at most five selected edges. A special vertex belongs to only one group. An ordinary line through \\(p\\) covers none, by the same unique-join argument. If an ordinary line \\(\\ell\\not\\ni p\\) covers selected edges, it meets each contributing line in at most one point, so covers at most one selected edge per contributing group. Every such group has a point \\(y\\in Y_i\\cap\\ell\\) whose join to \\(p\\) has direction in \\(S_i\\). The five-group bound of Proposition 5 limits these groups to five.</p><p>At most \\(h_p-1\\) additional vertices are available. They cover at most \\(5(h_p-1)\\) of the \\(5h_p\\) selected edges, a contradiction.</p><p>All projective centers are excluded. Hence \\(\\tau(H)\\ge q+1\\). Conversely, every edge of the nonempty intersecting \\(H\\) is itself a cover of size \\(q+1\\), so\n\\[\n\\tau(H)=q+1.\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(s_0\\) large enough for Proposition 4. For each eligible \\(s\\), fix one successful \\(Y\\), and choose \\(n_0(s)\\ge3\\) exceeding all resulting degree thresholds. The construction then works for every odd \\(n\\ge n_0(s)\\), proving the theorem.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 1.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(s_0\\) and \\(n_0(s)\\) be as in Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(s\\ge s_0\\) be prime with \\(s\\equiv2\\pmod3\\), let \\(n\\ge n_0(s)\\) be odd, and put \\(r=s^n+1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An \\(r\\)-edge-coloring assigns exactly one of \\(r\\) colors to each edge.</p><p class=\"li\">A monochromatic tree cover is a family of monochromatic trees whose vertex sets cover the graph. Trees may overlap, and singleton trees are allowed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a finite simple complete graph with an \\(r\\)-edge-coloring for which the minimum number of monochromatic trees needed to cover its vertices is exactly \\(r\\). The same minimum holds with connected monochromatic subgraphs or maximal monochromatic components in place of trees.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take \\(H\\) from Theorem 1, with parts \\(V_1,\\ldots,V_r\\). For \\(e\\in E(H)\\), let \\(x_i(e)\\) be the unique vertex of \\(e\\cap V_i\\).</p><p>Form a complete graph \\(K\\) with vertex set \\(E(H)\\). For every pair of distinct hyperedges \\(e,f\\), choose one index \\(i\\) with\n\\[\nx_i(e)=x_i(f),\n\\]\nand color \\(ef\\) by \\(i\\). Such an index exists because \\(H\\) is intersecting. Choose only one color even if \\(e,f\\) meet in several parts.</p><p>Along every color-\\(i\\) path, \\(x_i\\) is constant. Thus a connected color-\\(i\\) subgraph determines a vertex of \\(V_i\\) belonging to every hyperedge indexed by its vertices. For a singleton, choose any color and the corresponding vertex of its hyperedge.</p><p>If \\(t\\) connected monochromatic subgraphs cover \\(V(K)\\), the at most \\(t\\) hypergraph vertices so chosen meet every edge of \\(H\\). Therefore\n\\[\nt\\ge\\tau(H)=r.\n\\]</p><p>Conversely, fix a vertex of \\(K\\). The \\(r\\) monochromatic stars centered there, one for each color and allowing singleton stars, cover all vertices. They give a tree cover of size at most \\(r\\). The lower bound applies to trees and maximal monochromatic components as well, and the stars can be enlarged to maximal components. All three minima are exactly \\(r\\).</p><p>Consequently these examples refute Gyárfás’s monochromatic tree-cover conjecture. They admit no cover by \\(r-1\\) connected monochromatic subgraphs even without diameter restrictions, so they also refute the bounded-diameter strengthening cited in the paper as [26, Conjecture 6] and the \\(\\alpha=1\\) case of [12, Conjecture 2.8], since \\(\\alpha(K)=1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Theorem 2.1, Uniform incidence saving", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions and assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions and assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">For projective points \\(P\\) and projective lines \\(L\\) over \\(\\mathbb F_q\\), write\n\\[\nI(P,L)=|\\{(p,\\ell)\\in P\\times L:p\\in\\ell\\}|.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Every proper subfield of \\(\\mathbb F_q\\) has order at most \\(q^{1/3}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist absolute constants \\(\\kappa\\in(0,1/10)\\) and \\(Q\\) such that, whenever \\(q\\ge Q\\) and\n\\[\n|P|,|L|\\le q^{1+\\kappa},\n\\]\none has\n\\[\nI(P,L)\\le q^{3/2-\\kappa}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose the proposition is false. For positive \\(\\kappa_j\\to0\\), choose admissible counterexamples with \\(q_j\\to\\infty\\). Put \\(U=\\max(2,q_j^{\\kappa_j})\\), suppress the index, and obtain\n\\[\n|P|,|L|\\le Uq,\\qquad\nI(P,L)\\ge q^{3/2}/U,\\qquad U=q^{o(1)}.\n\\]</p><p>Prune the incidence graph. On either side, counting ordered pairs on the opposite side and using unique joins and intersections gives\n\\[\n\\sum_v d(v)^2\\le2U^2q^2.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nH=16U^3q^{1/2},\\qquad d=\\frac{q^{1/2}}{8U^2}.\n\\]\nDelete vertices whose initial degree exceeds \\(H\\). At most\n\\[\n\\frac{4U^2q^2}{H}=\\frac{q^{3/2}}{4U}\n\\]\nincidences are lost. Next iteratively delete vertices of current degree less than \\(d\\). There are at most \\(2Uq\\) original vertices, so this loses at most another \\(q^{3/2}/(4U)\\) incidences. The surviving sets, again denoted \\(P,L\\), satisfy\n\\[\nI(P,L)\\ge\\frac{q^{3/2}}{2U},\\qquad\n\\frac{q}{32U^4}\\le |P|,|L|\\le Uq,\\qquad\nd\\le d(v)\\le H.\n\\]</p><p>For every projective line \\(\\ell\\), each point of \\(P\\cap\\ell\\) is incident with at least \\(d-1\\) lines of \\(L\\) different from \\(\\ell\\), and these lines are distinct for different points. Hence, for large \\(q\\),\n\\[\n|P\\cap\\ell|\\le\\frac{|L|}{d-1}\\le16U^3q^{1/2}=H.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(x\\in P\\), let \\(N(x)\\) consist of points of \\(P\\setminus\\{x\\}\\) joined to \\(x\\) by a line of \\(L\\). Distinct lines through \\(x\\) have disjoint point sets away from \\(x\\), so\n\\[\n|N(x)|\\ge d(d-1)\\ge\\frac{q}{128U^4}.\n\\]\nPut \\(t_\\ell=|N(x)\\cap\\ell|\\). Then\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell\\in L}t_\\ell\\ge d|N(x)|\n\\ge\\frac{q^{3/2}}{1024U^6}.\n\\]\nLines with \\(t_\\ell\\le2\\) contribute at most \\(2Uq\\), eventually at most half this lower bound. Thus\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell:t_\\ell\\ge3}t_\\ell\n\\ge\\frac{q^{3/2}}{2048U^6}.\n\\]\nSince \\(t(t-1)(t-2)\\ge(2/9)t^3\\) for integers \\(t\\ge3\\), Hölder’s inequality gives at least\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell\\in L}t_\\ell(t_\\ell-1)(t_\\ell-2)\n\\ge\\frac{2}{9|L|^2}\n\\left(\\sum_{\\ell:t_\\ell\\ge3}t_\\ell\\right)^3\n\\ge\\frac{q^{5/2}}{9\\cdot2^{32}U^{20}}\n\\]\nordered collinear triples of distinct points of \\(N(x)\\) on a line of \\(L\\).</p><p>Each triple admits at least\n\\[\n\\frac{q}{128U^4}-H\\ge\\frac{q}{256U^4}\n\\]\nchoices of a fourth point of \\(N(x)\\) off its line. Summing over \\(x\\), there are at least\n\\[\n\\frac{q^{9/2}}{9\\cdot2^{45}U^{28}}\n\\]\nordered configurations \\((x,p_1,p_2,p_3,p_4)\\), where the first three anchors are distinct and collinear on a line of \\(L\\), the fourth is off that line, and all anchors lie in \\(N(x)\\).</p><p>There are at most\n\\[\n|L|H^3|P|\\le2^{12}U^{11}q^{7/2}\n\\]\npossible ordered anchor quadruples. Some quadruple therefore has at least\n\\[\n\\frac{c_0q}{U^{39}},\\qquad c_0=\\frac1{9\\cdot2^{57}},\n\\]\nwitnesses \\(x\\). Discard witnesses on the common line of \\(p_1,p_2,p_3\\) and on the joins of \\(p_1,p_4\\) and \\(p_2,p_4\\). At most \\(3H\\) witnesses are discarded, leaving at least \\(c_0q/(2U^{39})\\).</p><p>Choose projective coordinates with\n\\[\np_1=(1:0:0),\\quad p_2=(0:1:0),\\quad\np_3=(1:1:0),\\quad p_4=(0:0:1).\n\\]\nRepresentatives of the first two anchors span their line; the third permits rescaling those representatives, and the fourth completes a basis. The discarded lines are the coordinate line at infinity and the two coordinate axes through \\(p_4\\). Each remaining witness is \\((a:b:1)\\) with \\(a,b\\ne0\\).</p><p>Let \\(G\\) be the set of these pairs and \\(A,B\\) its projections. For a witness, the joins to \\(p_2,p_1,p_3,p_4\\), respectively, are\n\\[\nX=aZ,\\qquad Y=bZ,\\qquad\nX-Y=(a-b)Z,\\qquad X=(a/b)Y.\n\\]\nAll belong to \\(L\\), and every anchor has degree at most \\(H\\). Consequently, with \\(D,J\\) as in Lemma 1.2,\n\\[\n|A|,|B|,|D|,|J|\\le H,\\qquad\n|G|\\ge\\frac{c_0q}{2U^{39}}.\n\\]\nTake\n\\[\nn=q^{1/2},\\qquad V=\\frac2{c_0}U^{39}.\n\\]\nLemma 1.2 gives \\(S\\subseteq\\mathbb F_q^*\\) with\n\\[\n\\frac{3q^{1/2}}{8V^2}\\le|S|\\le Vq^{1/2},\n\\qquad\n|S-S|,\\ |S/S|\\le2^{20}V^{22}q^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThus \\(|S|=q^{1/2+o(1)}\\), and \\(K=2^{23}V^{24}\\) satisfies\n\\[\nK=q^{o(1)},\\qquad |S-S|,\\ |S/S|\\le K|S|.\n\\]\nThis contradicts Lemma 1.4. The negation of the proposition would produce such a sequence, so suitable absolute \\(\\kappa,Q\\) exist.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.2, Triangle, covering, and multiple-sum estimates", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions and notation.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and notation.\nAll sets below are finite subsets of an abelian group. For a nonnegative integer \\(j\\), \\(jY\\) denotes the sum of \\(j\\) copies of \\(Y\\), with \\(0Y=\\{0\\}\\).</p><p>Statements.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(Z\\ne\\varnothing\\), then\n\\[\n|X-Y|\\,|Z|\\le |X-Z|\\,|Z-Y|.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(Y\\ne\\varnothing\\) and \\(|X-Y|\\le M|Y|\\), then \\(X\\) is contained in the union of at most \\(M\\) translates of \\(Y-Y\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(Y\\ne\\varnothing\\) and \\(|Y-Y|\\le M|Y|\\), then, for all nonnegative integers \\(u,v\\),\n\\[\n|uY-vY|\\le M^{u+v}|Y|.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For the triangle inequality, choose one representation \\(d=x_d-y_d\\) for each \\(d\\in X-Y\\). The map\n\\[\n(d,z)\\longmapsto(x_d-z,z-y_d)\n\\]\nfrom \\((X-Y)\\times Z\\) into \\((X-Z)\\times(Z-Y)\\) is injective: summing the two coordinates recovers \\(d\\), after which the chosen representation recovers \\(z\\).</p><p>For the covering statement, take a maximal set \\(T\\subseteq X\\) for which the translates \\(t-Y\\), \\(t\\in T\\), are pairwise disjoint. Their union is contained in \\(X-Y\\), so \\(|T|\\le M\\). Maximality implies that for every \\(x\\in X\\), some \\(x-Y\\) intersects \\(t-Y\\), whence \\(x\\in t+(Y-Y)\\).</p><p>For the multiple-sum bound, put \\(Q_0=-Y\\) and choose a nonempty \\(W\\subseteq Y\\) minimizing\n\\[\n\\mu=\\frac{|W+Q_0|}{|W|}.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\mu\\le M\\), and every \\(W'\\subseteq W\\), including the empty set, satisfies\n\\[\n|W'+Q_0|\\ge\\mu|W'|.\n\\]\nWe claim that for every finite set \\(E_0\\),\n\\[\n|W+Q_0+E_0|\\le\\mu|W+E_0|.\n\\]\nThis is immediate if \\(E_0\\) is empty. Otherwise order its elements \\(e_1,\\ldots,e_k\\). When \\(W+e_i\\) is added to the preceding translates, let\n\\[\nW_i'=\\left\\{w\\in W:w+e_i\\in\\bigcup_{j&lt;i}(W+e_j)\\right\\}.\n\\]\nIt contributes exactly \\(|W|-|W_i'|\\) new elements. Moreover, \\(W_i'+Q_0+e_i\\) is contained in the preceding enlarged translates. The contribution of \\(W+Q_0+e_i\\) is therefore at most\n\\[\n|W+Q_0|-|W_i'+Q_0|\n\\le\\mu(|W|-|W_i'|).\n\\]\nSumming proves the claim. Iteration gives\n\\[\n|W+jQ_0|\\le\\mu^j|W|\n\\]\nfor every nonnegative integer \\(j\\).</p><p>Apply the triangle inequality to \\(uQ_0,vQ_0\\) with intermediate set \\(-W\\):\n\\[\n|uQ_0-vQ_0|\\,|W|\n\\le |W+uQ_0|\\,|W+vQ_0|\n\\le\\mu^{u+v}|W|^2.\n\\]\nNegation preserves cardinality, so\n\\[\n|uY-vY|\\le M^{u+v}|Y|.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.3, Simultaneous difference and quotient extraction", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(F\\) is a field, \\(A,B\\subseteq F^*\\) are finite, and \\(G\\subseteq A\\times B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nD=\\{a-b:(a,b)\\in G\\},\\qquad\nJ=\\{a/b:(a,b)\\in G\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For real numbers \\(n&gt;0\\) and \\(V\\ge2\\), suppose\n\\[\n|A|,|B|,|D|,|J|\\le Vn,\\qquad |G|\\ge n^2/V.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(S\\subseteq A\\) such that\n\\[\n\\frac{3n}{8V^2}\\le |S|\\le Vn\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|S-S|,\\ |S/S|\\le 2^{20}V^{22}n.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Regard \\(G\\) as a bipartite graph, and put\n\\[\n\\tau=(512V^8)^{-1}.\n\\]\nCall an ordered pair \\((a,a_2)\\in A^2\\) bad if it has fewer than \\(\\tau n\\) common neighbors in \\(B\\).</p><p>Vertices of \\(B\\) with fewer than \\(n/(2V^2)\\) neighbors account for at most \\(n^2/(2V)\\) edges. Since every vertex of \\(B\\) has at most \\(Vn\\) neighbors, at least \\(n/(2V^2)\\) vertices have at least \\(n/(2V^2)\\) neighbors.</p><p>Summed over all \\(b\\in B\\), the number of bad ordered pairs in the neighborhood of \\(b\\) is at most\n\\[\n|A|^2\\tau n\\le V^2\\tau n^3.\n\\]\nAveraging over the large neighborhoods gives a neighborhood \\(A_0\\) of size at least \\(n/(2V^2)\\) in which the number of bad ordered pairs is at most\n\\[\n2V^4\\tau n^2=\\frac{n^2}{256V^4}\n\\le\\frac{|A_0|^2}{64}\\le\\frac{|A_0|^2}{16}.\n\\]\nLet \\(S\\) consist of those \\(a\\in A_0\\) with at most \\(|A_0|/4\\) bad partners in \\(A_0\\). At most \\(|A_0|/4\\) elements are discarded, giving\n\\[\n|S|\\ge 3|A_0|/4\\ge3n/(8V^2).\n\\]</p><p>For every \\(a,a'\\in S\\), at least \\(|A_0|/2\\) elements \\(a_2\\in A_0\\) form nonbad pairs with both endpoints. Each such \\(a_2\\) gives at least \\((\\tau n)^2\\) ordered walks\n\\[\na,b_1,a_2,b_2,a'\n\\]\nwhose four edges belong to \\(G\\). Repeated vertices are allowed. Every ordered endpoint pair in \\(S^2\\) therefore has at least\n\\[\n\\frac{|A_0|}{2}(\\tau n)^2\\ge\\frac{n^3}{2^{20}V^{18}}\n\\]\nsuch walks.</p><p>For one walk, write\n\\[\nd_1=a-b_1,\\quad d_2=a_2-b_1,\\quad\nd_3=a_2-b_2,\\quad d_4=a'-b_2.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\na-a'=d_1-d_2+d_3-d_4.\n\\]\nFor fixed endpoints, the four differences determine the walk uniquely. Choose one ordered endpoint pair for each element of \\(S-S\\). The collections of difference quadruples for different differences are disjoint. Counting in \\(D^4\\) gives\n\\[\n|S-S|\\le\n\\frac{|D|^4}{n^3/(2^{20}V^{18})}\n\\le2^{20}V^{22}n.\n\\]</p><p>The same walks give quotient control on the same set \\(S\\). Put\n\\[\nj_1=a/b_1,\\quad j_2=a_2/b_1,\\quad\nj_3=a_2/b_2,\\quad j_4=a'/b_2.\n\\]\nAll are nonzero, and\n\\[\na/a'=\\frac{j_1j_3}{j_2j_4}.\n\\]\nFrom a fixed initial \\(a\\) and these quotients one recovers\n\\[\nb_1=a/j_1,\\quad a_2=j_2b_1,\\quad\nb_2=a_2/j_3,\\quad a'=j_4b_2.\n\\]\nChoosing one endpoint pair for each element of \\(S/S\\) and counting in \\(J^4\\) proves the other bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.4, Uniform scalar coverings", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(B\\) is a nonempty finite subset of a field \\(F\\), \\(E\\subseteq B\\), and \\(K\\ge2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n\\[\n|B-B|\\le K|B|,\\qquad\n|B-eB|\\le2K^3|B|\\quad(e\\in E).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">An expression in \\(E\\) is built from members of \\(E\\cup\\{0,1\\}\\) using binary addition, subtraction, multiplication, and unary negation and inversion. Each operation counts once; inversion is allowed only for nonzero arguments.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nC_0=4,\\qquad C_{m+1}=3C_m+5,\\qquad\nC_m=\\frac{13\\cdot3^m-5}{2}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(\\lambda\\in F\\) has an expression in \\(E\\) using at most \\(m\\) operations, then \\(\\lambda B\\) is contained in at most \\(K^{C_m}\\) translates of \\(B-B\\). In particular,\n\\[\n|E+\\lambda E|\\le|B+\\lambda B|\\le K^{C_m+3}|B|.\n\\]\nThe bounds are uniform over all such expressions and choices of their entries from \\(E\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(H=B-B\\), so \\(H=-H\\) and \\(0\\in H\\). Integer multiples inside sumsets in this proof denote iterated sums.</p><p>By Lemma 1.1,\n\\[\n|2H-B|=|2B-3B|\\le K^5|B|.\n\\]\nThe covering estimate therefore gives \\(T_*\\subseteq F\\), \\(|T_*|\\le K^5\\), such that\n\\[\n2H\\subseteq T_*+H.\n\\]</p><p>Every \\(e\\in E\\) has a covering\n\\[\neB\\subseteq T_e+H,\\qquad |T_e|\\le2K^3\\le K^4.\n\\]\nThe scalars \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) have coverings by one translate of \\(H\\): use \\(\\{0\\}\\subseteq H\\) and \\(B\\subseteq b_0+H\\) for \\(b_0\\in B\\). This proves the case \\(m=0\\).</p><p>Suppose\n\\[\n\\lambda B\\subseteq T+H,\\quad \\mu B\\subseteq U+H,\\quad\n|T|\\le K^a,\\quad |U|\\le K^b.\n\\]\nNegation replaces \\(T\\) by \\(-T\\). For addition and subtraction,\n\\[\n(\\lambda\\pm\\mu)B\n\\subseteq T\\pm U+2H\n\\subseteq T\\pm U+T_*+H,\n\\]\nusing at most \\(K^{a+b+5}\\) translates. For multiplication,\n\\[\n\\lambda\\mu B\n\\subseteq\\lambda U+\\lambda H\n\\subseteq\\lambda U+T-T+T_*+H,\n\\]\nusing at most \\(K^{b+2a+5}\\) translates, also when \\(\\lambda=0\\).</p><p>If \\(\\lambda\\ne0\\), then\n\\[\n|\\lambda B-B|\n\\le |T|\\,|H-B|\n=|T|\\,|B-2B|\n\\le K^{a+3}|B|.\n\\]\nScaling by \\(\\lambda^{-1}\\) and negating gives\n\\[\n|\\lambda^{-1}B-B|\\le K^{a+3}|B|.\n\\]\nThe covering estimate covers \\(\\lambda^{-1}B\\) by at most \\(K^{a+3}\\) translates of \\(H\\).</p><p>Every immediate subexpression of an expression with at most \\(m+1\\) operations has at most \\(m\\) operations. The preceding bounds therefore use at most \\(K^{3C_m+5}\\) translates, proving the induction.</p><p>Finally, if \\(\\lambda B\\subseteq T+H\\), then\n\\[\n|B+\\lambda B|\n\\le |T|\\,|B+H|\n=|T|\\,|2B-B|\n\\le K^{C_m+3}|B|.\n\\]\nThe inclusion \\(E\\subseteq B\\) gives the remaining inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma 2.5, Exclusion of an intermediate approximate subfield", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.\nAlong a sequence \\(q\\to\\infty\\), \\(K=q^{o(1)}\\) means \\(\\log K/\\log q\\to0\\), and \\(|S|=q^{1/2+o(1)}\\) means \\(\\log|S|/\\log q\\to1/2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is no sequence of prime powers \\(q\\to\\infty\\), each satisfying the proper-subfield hypothesis of Proposition 1, together with \\(S\\subseteq\\mathbb F_q^*\\) and \\(K\\ge2\\), such that\n\\[\n|S|=q^{1/2+o(1)},\\qquad K=q^{o(1)},\\qquad\n|S-S|,\\ |S/S|\\le K|S|.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose such a sequence exists, and put \\(s=|S|\\). The \\(s\\) sets \\(S/a\\), \\(a\\in S\\), each have size \\(s\\), and their union is contained in \\(S/S\\). Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{a,b\\in S}|S/a\\cap S/b|\n\\ge\\frac{s^4}{|S/S|}\\ge\\frac{s^3}{K}.\n\\]\nSome \\(b\\in S\\) therefore satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum_{a\\in S}|S/a\\cap S/b|\\ge s^2/K.\n\\]\nAt least \\(s/(2K)\\) values of \\(a\\in S\\) have\n\\[\n|S/a\\cap S/b|\\ge s/(2K).\n\\]\nLet \\(E\\) consist of \\(e=a/b\\) for these values, and put \\(B=S/b\\). Then\n\\[\nE\\subseteq B,\\qquad |B|=s,\\qquad |E|\\ge s/(2K),\n\\]\nand, multiplying the intersections by \\(a\\),\n\\[\n|S\\cap eS|\\ge s/(2K)\\quad(e\\in E).\n\\]</p><p>For \\(Z=S\\cap eS\\), Lemma 1.1 gives\n\\[\n|S-eS|\n\\le\\frac{|S-Z|\\,|Z-eS|}{|Z|}\n\\le\\frac{(Ks)^2}{s/(2K)}\n=2K^3s.\n\\]\nAfter scaling,\n\\[\n|B-B|\\le Ks,\\qquad |B-eB|\\le2K^3s.\n\\]\nThus Lemma 1.3 applies.</p><p>Write \\(t=|E|=q^{1/2+o(1)}\\), so \\(t\\ge2\\) for large \\(q\\), and define\n\\[\nR_0=\n\\left\\{\\frac{x-x'}{y-y'}:\nx,x',y,y'\\in E,\\ y\\ne y'\\right\\}.\n\\]\nEvery member of \\(R_0\\) has an expression in \\(E\\) using at most four operations. For \\(r,r'\\in R_0\\), each of \\(r+r'\\) and \\(rr'\\) has an expression using at most nine operations. For either candidate \\(\\lambda\\), Lemma 1.3 gives\n\\[\n|E+\\lambda E|\\le K^{C_9+3}s&lt;t^2\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(q\\), uniformly in \\(r,r'\\). Indeed, the ratio to \\(t^2\\) is at most \\(4K^{C_9+5}/s\\), which tends to zero.</p><p>The map \\((x,y)\\mapsto x+\\lambda y\\) on \\(E^2\\) is therefore not injective. A collision between distinct pairs has \\(y\\ne y'\\), and gives\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{x'-x}{y-y'}\\in R_0.\n\\]\nThus \\(R_0\\) is closed under addition and multiplication. It contains \\(0,1\\), and its definition gives closure under negation and reciprocals of nonzero elements. It is a subfield of \\(\\mathbb F_q\\).</p><p>For distinct \\(e_0,e_1\\in E\\), the map\n\\[\ne\\longmapsto\\frac{e-e_0}{e_1-e_0}\n\\]\ninjects \\(E\\) into \\(R_0\\). Hence \\(|R_0|\\ge t&gt;q^{1/3}\\) for large \\(q\\), so the proper-subfield hypothesis forces \\(R_0=\\mathbb F_q\\).</p><p>For \\(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb F_q\\), let \\(N_\\lambda\\) count ordered quadruples \\((x,y,x',y')\\in E^4\\) satisfying\n\\[\nx+\\lambda y=x'+\\lambda y'.\n\\]\nThere are \\(t^2\\) identical pairs giving collisions for every \\(\\lambda\\). If \\(y\\ne y'\\), exactly one \\(\\lambda\\) gives a collision; if \\(y=y'\\) but \\(x\\ne x'\\), none does. Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac1q\\sum_{\\lambda\\in\\mathbb F_q}N_\\lambda\n=t^2+\\frac{t^4-t^3}{q}\n\\le t^2+\\frac{t^4}{q}.\n\\]\nFor some \\(\\lambda\\), Cauchy–Schwarz on the fibers gives\n\\[\n|E+\\lambda E|\n\\ge\\frac{t^4}{t^2+t^4/q}\n\\ge\\frac12\\min(t^2,q)\n=q^{1-o(1)}.\n\\]\nBut \\(R_0=\\mathbb F_q\\), so this \\(\\lambda\\) has a four-operation representation. Lemma 1.3 bounds the same sumset by\n\\[\nK^{C_4+3}s=q^{1/2+o(1)},\n\\]\na contradiction. The expression length remains bounded; no iteration of field generation is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, Line covers after random deletions", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions and assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions and assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(q\\to\\infty\\) through prime powers such that every proper subfield of \\(\\mathbb F_q\\) has order at most \\(q^{1/3}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">View \\(\\mathbb F_q^2\\) inside its projective completion. A pencil is the family of affine lines through a projective point: it has \\(q+1\\) members for a finite center and \\(q\\) members for a center at infinity.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a positive integer \\(D\\), \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), and \\(0&lt;\\rho&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of at most \\(Dq^D\\) subsets of \\(\\mathbb F_q^2\\), called shapes, such that every \\(F\\in\\mathcal F\\) has at most \\(Dq\\) points, and for every affine line \\(\\ell\\), either \\(F=\\ell\\) or \\(|F\\cap\\ell|\\le D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At most \\(Dq\\) independent trials each produce a random list of at most \\(D\\) shapes. Delete all points on listed shapes, leaving \\(X\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For every point \\(x\\) and trial, the probability that the trial deletes \\(x\\) is at most \\(D/q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The survival probability is uniform:\n\\[\n\\Pr(x\\in X)=\\delta_q\\ge\\delta.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For every affine line \\(\\ell\\), the sum over trials of probabilities that the trial lists the exact shape \\(\\ell\\) is at most \\(D/q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X^\\ell\\) be the surviving set when exact occurrences of \\(\\ell\\) are ignored, while all other listed shapes remain deleted.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With probability tending to one:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(|X^\\ell\\cap\\ell|\\ge\\delta q/2\\) for every affine line \\(\\ell\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every set of at most \\(q\\) affine lines covering all but at most \\(2q\\) points of \\(X\\) contains at least \\((1-\\rho)q\\) lines through a common projective point.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(\\kappa\\in(0,1/10)\\) from Proposition 1 and put \\(\\epsilon=\\kappa/10\\). Constants may depend on the fixed parameters, but not on \\(q\\), the deletion distributions, or a subsequently chosen cover.</p><p>First establish uniform point and line counts. Expose the independent trials one at a time. Changing one trial changes \\(|X|\\) by at most \\(2D^2q\\). The independent-trial exposure bound and bounded-drift concentration estimate imported in the paper from [27, Lemmas A.2–A.3], applied with at most \\(Dq\\) trials and deviation \\(q^{3/2+\\epsilon}\\), give\n\\[\n\\Pr\\bigl(\\bigl||X|-\\delta_qq^2\\bigr|\\ge q^{3/2+\\epsilon}\\bigr)\n\\le2\\exp\\left(-\\frac{q^{2\\epsilon}}{32D^5}\\right).\n\\]\nIf there are no trials, the count is deterministic; equivalently, add a dummy trial.</p><p>For \\(|X^\\ell\\cap\\ell|\\), changing one trial changes the count by at most \\(2D^2\\), because exact copies of \\(\\ell\\) are ignored. The same imported estimates give\n\\[\n\\Pr\\bigl(\\bigl||X^\\ell\\cap\\ell|-\\mathbb E|X^\\ell\\cap\\ell|\\bigr|\n\\ge q^{1/2+\\epsilon}\\bigr)\n\\le2\\exp\\left(-\\frac{q^{2\\epsilon}}{32D^5}\\right).\n\\]\nA restored point requires some trial to have listed \\(\\ell\\), an event of probability at most \\(D/q\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\delta_qq\\le\\mathbb E|X^\\ell\\cap\\ell|\\le\\delta_qq+D.\n\\]\nA union bound over the \\(q(q+1)\\) affine lines yields, with probability tending to one,\n\\[\n|X|=\\delta_qq^2+O_D(q^{3/2+\\epsilon}),\n\\]\n\\[\n|X^\\ell\\cap\\ell|=\\delta_qq+O_D(q^{1/2+\\epsilon})\n\\quad\\text{for every }\\ell.\n\\]\nThis proves the first conclusion and also gives\n\\[\n|X\\cap\\ell|\\le\\delta_qq+O_D(q^{1/2+\\epsilon}).\n\\]</p><p>Next establish a simultaneous event for short line lists. Put\n\\[\nh=10(D+1),\\qquad H_0=2Dh,\\qquad\nt=\\lceil A\\log q\\rceil,\n\\]\nwhere \\(A\\) will be fixed below. Consider ordered tuples of \\(2t\\) distinct affine lines, divided into two lists of length \\(t\\). A tuple is admissible if its cross-list intersections are \\(t^2\\) distinct finite points forming \\(P_0\\), and\n\\[\n|F\\cap P_0|\\le H_0\n\\]\nfor every \\(F\\in\\mathcal F\\) which is not a tuple line.</p><p>For a fixed admissible tuple, ignore deletion shapes equal to tuple lines, and let \\(Y_0\\) count survivors in \\(P_0\\). Omission is a deterministic operation on each trial, so independence remains, and\n\\[\n\\mathbb EY_0\\ge\\delta t^2.\n\\]\nEach trial deletes at most \\(C_0=DH_0\\) points of \\(P_0\\), and its contribution is nonempty with probability at most \\(Dt^2/q\\).</p><p>For the exposure martingale, fix the past, let \\(g_i(w)\\) be the conditional expected final \\(Y_0\\) for current outcome \\(w\\), and let \\(g_i(\\varnothing)\\) be the hypothetical value when this trial deletes nothing on \\(P_0\\). Independence leaves the future law unchanged, so\n\\[\n|g_i(w)-g_i(\\varnothing)|\\le C_0,\n\\]\nwith difference zero for an empty contribution. The martingale increment is bounded by \\(2C_0\\), and its conditional variance is at most\n\\[\nC_0^2Dt^2/q.\n\\]\nThe sum of conditional variances is at most \\(D^2C_0^2t^2\\). Lemma 2.1 gives\n\\[\n\\Pr(Y_0&lt;\\delta t^2/2)\\le2e^{-c_0t^2},\n\\qquad\nc_0=\\min\\left\\{\\frac{\\delta^2}{16D^2C_0^2},\n\\frac{\\delta}{16C_0}\\right\\}&gt;0.\n\\]\nThere are at most \\((q(q+1))^{2t}\\) tuples. The logarithm of the union bound is\n\\[\n-c_0t^2+4t\\log q+O(t/q)+O(1)\n=(-c_0A^2+4A+o(1))(\\log q)^2.\n\\]\nChoose \\(A&gt;5/c_0\\). With probability tending to one,\n\\[\nY_0\\ge\\delta t^2/2\n\\]\nsimultaneously for every admissible tuple.</p><p>Fix a realization satisfying both simultaneous events. Suppose the second conclusion fails. Remove from an offending cover every line that occurred as a listed deletion shape. Such lines contain no point of \\(X\\). Let \\(L\\) be the remaining set, \\(m=|L|\\), and\n\\[\nn_p=|\\{\\ell\\in L:p\\in\\ell\\}|,\\qquad\nE_L=\\sum_{x\\in X}(n_x-1)_+.\n\\]\nThe count of covered survivors and the uniform line upper bound give\n\\[\nm\\bigl(\\delta_qq+O_D(q^{1/2+\\epsilon})\\bigr)\n\\ge\\delta_qq^2-O_D(q^{3/2+\\epsilon})-2q.\n\\]\nSince \\(m\\le q\\) and \\(\\delta_q\\ge\\delta\\),\n\\[\nm=q-O_{D,\\delta}(q^{1/2+\\epsilon}),\n\\qquad E_L=O_D(q^{3/2+\\epsilon}).\n\\]\nThe second bound follows by subtracting the number of covered survivors from \\(\\sum_{\\ell\\in L}|X\\cap\\ell|\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\nR=\\max_{p\\in\\mathbb P^2(\\mathbb F_q)}n_p.\n\\]\nFailure of the pencil conclusion gives \\(R\\le(1-\\rho)q\\). We find an admissible tuple in \\(L\\) with too few surviving cross-intersections.</p><p>If \\(R\\le q^{1/2+\\kappa/4}\\), put \\(T=q^{1/2-\\kappa/3}\\). The number of ordered distinct line pairs meeting in \\(X\\) is\n\\[\n\\sum_{x\\in X}n_x(n_x-1).\n\\]\nPoints with \\(n_x&lt;T\\) contribute at most\n\\[\nTE_L=O(q^{2-7\\kappa/30}).\n\\]\nFor \\(P=\\{x\\in X:n_x\\ge T\\}\\),\n\\[\n|P|\\le\\frac{E_L}{T-1}\n=O(q^{1+13\\kappa/30})\\le q^{1+\\kappa}.\n\\]\nProposition 1 gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{x\\in P}n_x\\le q^{3/2-\\kappa}.\n\\]\nTheir ordered-pair contribution is at most\n\\[\nR\\sum_{x\\in P}n_x\\le q^{2-3\\kappa/4}.\n\\]\nThe fraction of ordered distinct line pairs meeting in \\(X\\) is therefore\n\\[\nO(q^{-7\\kappa/30}+q^{-3\\kappa/4})=o(1).\n\\]</p><p>Sample \\(2t\\) distinct lines uniformly from \\(L\\). The expected number of cross pairs meeting in \\(X\\) is \\(o(t^2)\\); Markov’s inequality makes it less than \\(\\delta t^2/2\\) with probability tending to one.</p><p>A specified pair is parallel with probability at most \\(R/(m-1)\\); a specified triple is concurrent with probability at most \\(R/(m-2)\\). The probability of any parallel pair or concurrent triple is\n\\[\nO((t^2+t^3)R/m)\n=O((\\log q)^3q^{-1/2+\\kappa/4})=o(1).\n\\]\nWithout these events, cross-intersections are finite and distinct.</p><p>Fix a shape \\(F\\). If \\(F\\) is not a tuple line but contains more than \\(H_0\\) distinct finite cross-intersections, each tuple line contains at most \\(D\\) points of \\(F\\). Greedily selecting one cross-intersection and discarding all cross-intersections on its two representing lines produces \\(h\\) cross pairs with \\(2h\\) distinct positions.</p><p>There are at most \\(mDR\\) ordered population line pairs, neither line equal to \\(F\\), meeting in \\(F\\). There are at most \\(t^{2h}\\) choices of the \\(h\\) cross pairs of positions. Writing \\((a)_j=a(a-1)\\cdots(a-j+1)\\), the probability of this shape violation is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{t^{2h}(mDR)^h}{(m)_{2h}}\n=O_{D,h}\\left(\\left(\\frac{t^2R}{m}\\right)^h\\right).\n\\]\nSumming over shapes gives\n\\[\nO((\\log q)^{2h}q^{D-h(1/2-\\kappa/4)})=o(1),\n\\]\nbecause\n\\[\nD-h(1/2-\\kappa/4)&lt;D-\\frac{19}{4}(D+1)&lt;0.\n\\]\nThus an admissible tuple with too few surviving cross-intersections exists in this regime.</p><p>If \\(R&gt;q^{1/2+\\kappa/4}\\), choose \\(p\\) with \\(n_p=R\\). Its \\(R\\) lines form the pencil, and its complement has\n\\[\nN=m-R\\ge\\rho q/2\n\\]\nlines. Sample the first list from the pencil and the second independently from the complement, without replacement within each list.</p><p>Every cross-intersection lies off \\(p\\). At a cross-intersection \\(x\\in X\\), exactly one pencil line passes through \\(x\\), and \\(n_x-1\\) complement lines do. At most \\(E_L\\) of the \\(RN\\) population cross pairs meet in \\(X\\), so their fraction is\n\\[\n\\frac{E_L}{RN}\n=O(q^{\\epsilon-\\kappa/4})\n=O(q^{-3\\kappa/20})=o(1).\n\\]\nAgain fewer than \\(\\delta t^2/2\\) sampled cross pairs meet in \\(X\\), with probability tending to one.</p><p>For finite \\(p\\), at most one pencil line meets a fixed complement line at infinity; for infinite \\(p\\), all cross-intersections are finite. The probability of any infinite intersection is at most \\(t^2/R\\). Equal cross-intersections must use the same pencil line, and two complement lines determine at most one such pencil line. The collision probability is \\(O(t^3/R)\\). Thus the total failure probability is\n\\[\nO((t^2+t^3)/R)\n=O((\\log q)^3q^{-1/2-\\kappa/4})=o(1).\n\\]</p><p>For a shape violation, the same greedy argument gives \\(h\\) cross pairs with disjoint positions. There are at most \\(DN\\) population cross pairs, neither line equal to \\(F\\), whose intersection lies in \\(F\\): each complement line meets \\(F\\) in at most \\(D\\) points off \\(p\\), each determining at most one pencil line. The violation probability for \\(F\\) is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{t^{2h}(DN)^h}{(R)_h(N)_h}\n=O_{D,h}\\left(\\left(\\frac{t^2}{R}\\right)^h\\right).\n\\]\nSumming over shapes gives\n\\[\nO((\\log q)^{2h}q^{D-h(1/2+\\kappa/4)})=o(1),\n\\]\nsince \\(D-h(1/2+\\kappa/4)&lt;D-5(D+1)&lt;0\\).</p><p>In either regime, for large \\(q\\) there is an admissible tuple from \\(L\\) with\n\\[\n|X\\cap P_0|&lt;\\delta t^2/2.\n\\]\nNo line of \\(L\\) occurred as a listed deletion shape. Ignoring exact occurrences of the tuple lines changes no deletions in this realization, so \\(Y_0=|X\\cap P_0|\\), contradicting the simultaneous event.</p><p>The cover and tuple may depend on the realized deletions: the event was established for every admissible tuple before the realization was fixed. Sampling inside \\(L\\) proves existence only; no concentration estimate is conditioned on the cover.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, Martingale concentration", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions.\nLet \\(Z_i\\), \\(1\\le i\\le n\\), be martingale differences for a filtration \\((\\mathcal A_i)_{i=0}^n\\). Suppose \\(M,V&gt;0\\) and deterministic \\(v_i\\ge0\\) satisfy, almost surely,\n\\[\n|Z_i|\\le M,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E(Z_i^2\\mid\\mathcal A_{i-1})\\le v_i,\\qquad\n\\sum_{i=1}^n v_i\\le V.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(u&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr\\left(\\left|\\sum_{i=1}^nZ_i\\right|\\ge u\\right)\n\\le2\\exp\\left[-\\frac14\\min\\left\\{\\frac{u^2}{V},\\frac{u}{M}\\right\\}\\right].\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(|z|\\le1\\), \\(e^z\\le1+z+z^2\\). Thus, if \\(|\\theta|\\le M^{-1}\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(e^{\\theta Z_i}\\mid\\mathcal A_{i-1})\n\\le1+\\theta^2v_i\\le e^{\\theta^2v_i}.\n\\]\nIteration and Markov’s inequality bound the upper tail by\n\\[\n\\exp(\\theta^2V-\\theta u)\n\\]\nfor \\(0&lt;\\theta\\le M^{-1}\\). Take\n\\[\n\\theta=\\min\\{u/(2V),1/M\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(u\\le2V/M\\), the exponent is \\(-u^2/(4V)\\); otherwise it is at most \\(-u/(2M)\\). Apply the same argument to \\(-Z_i\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Theorem 4.1, Compatible translates from independent pools", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a positive integer \\(D\\), and let \\(q\\to\\infty\\) through prime powers.</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(q\\), let \\(n\\le Dq\\). For \\(1\\le i\\le n\\), let \\(W_i\\subseteq\\mathbb F_q^2\\) and let \\(D_i\\) be a set of directions, with\n\\[\n|W_i|\\le D,\\qquad |D_i|\\le D.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A direction is a one-dimensional linear subspace, including zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Each direction belongs to \\(D_i\\) for at most \\(D\\) indices.</p><p class=\"li\">For distinct \\(i,j\\), translates \\(b+W_i\\) and \\(b'+W_j\\) conflict if some \\(u\\in W_i\\), \\(v\\in W_j\\), and \\(d\\in D_i\\cup D_j\\) satisfy\n\\[\n(b+u)-(b'+v)\\in d.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a positive integer \\(K=K(D)\\) such that, if \\(b_{i,1},\\ldots,b_{i,K}\\) are independent uniform shifts in \\(\\mathbb F_q^2\\), independently for all \\(i\\), then with probability tending to one one can choose \\(k_i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}\\) for every \\(i\\) so that the selected translates \\(b_{i,k_i}+W_i\\) have no conflicts. Convergence is uniform over all data satisfying these bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>The case \\(n=0\\) is immediate. Fix \\(D\\) and initially a constant integer \\(K\\ge6\\).</p><p>Form a graph with candidate positions \\((i,k)\\) as vertices, partitioned into the pools. Candidates from different pools are adjacent precisely when their translates conflict. Candidate positions remain distinct vertices even when shifts coincide. An independent transversal is the required selection.</p><p>By Lemma 3.1, it suffices to exclude, with probability tending to one, nonempty index sets \\(I\\), \\(|I|=m\\), and sets \\(T\\) of at most \\(2m\\) candidate positions in these pools which dominate their union. Fix \\(I,T\\) before exposing shifts.</p><p>For every direction \\(d\\) in use, fix a nonzero linear functional\n\\[\n\\pi_d:\\mathbb F_q^2\\to\\mathbb F_q,\\qquad \\ker\\pi_d=d.\n\\]\nAfter exposing candidates \\(e\\in T\\), with source-pool index \\(j\\) and shift \\(b_e\\), call \\(b\\) feasible for target \\(i\\) if it avoids all equations\n\\[\n\\pi_d(b)=\\pi_d(b_e)+\\pi_d(v-u),\n\\quad u\\in W_i,\\ v\\in W_j,\\ d\\in D_i\\cup D_j.\n\\]\nImpose these equations also when \\(i=j\\), making feasibility more restrictive. A feasible candidate outside \\(T\\) prevents domination.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nC=2D^3,\\qquad c_1=\\frac1{4C},\\qquad\n\\alpha=\\frac1{2C}.\n\\]\nFor a target and an exposed candidate there are at most \\(C\\) equations, counting the two direction lists separately. Each excludes one affine line.</p><p>There are at most\n\\[\n\\binom nm\\le(eDq/m)^m\n\\]\nchoices of \\(I\\). For an absolute \\(C_*&gt;0\\), the number of choices of \\(T\\), \\(|T|\\le2m\\), is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=0}^{2m}\\binom{Km}{t}\\le(C_*K^2)^m.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(1\\le m\\le c_1q\\), condition on the shifts in \\(T\\). A fresh target shift is infeasible with probability at most\n\\[\nC|T|/q\\le2Cm/q\\le1/2.\n\\]\nAt least \\((K-2)m\\) positions remain unexposed and independent. The domination probability for fixed \\(I,T\\) is at most\n\\[\n(2Cm/q)^{(K-2)m}.\n\\]\nAfter counting \\(I,T\\), the probability for this \\(m\\) is at most\n\\[\n\\left[2CeDC_*K^2(2Cm/q)^{K-3}\\right]^m.\n\\]\nChoose \\(K\\) large enough that\n\\[\n2CeDC_*K^2\\,2^{-(K-3)}\\le1/2.\n\\]\nThe bracket is then at most \\(1/2\\) throughout this range and tends to zero for every fixed \\(m\\). A finite initial sum and a geometric tail give total probability \\(o(1)\\).</p><p>Now suppose \\(m&gt;c_1q\\). We first establish, for a constant \\(\\delta_1&gt;0\\) depending only on \\(D\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr\\left(\\sum_{i\\in I}|U_i^{\\mathrm{fin}}|&lt;\\delta_1mq^2\\right)\n\\le\\exp(-\\Omega_D(q\\log q)),\n\\]\nwhere \\(U_i^{\\mathrm{fin}}\\) is the set of shifts feasible for target \\(i\\) after exposing all of \\(T\\).</p><p>Expose \\(T\\) in chunks of at most \\(\\lfloor\\alpha q\\rfloor\\) entries. Since \\(|T|\\le2Dq\\), the number of chunks is at most\n\\[\nJ_0=\\left\\lceil\\frac{4D}{\\alpha}+1\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nIf \\(T\\) is empty, the density assertion is deterministic.</p><p>At the start of a chunk, condition on the past and freeze the currently feasible sets \\(U_i\\). Put\n\\[\nM=\\sum_{i\\in I}|U_i|.\n\\]\nFor a chunk entry \\(e\\), with source pool \\(j\\), let \\(Z_e\\) sum, over all associated forbidden equations and all targets \\(i\\in I\\), the number of \\(b\\in U_i\\) satisfying the equation. Count the \\(D_i\\) and \\(D_j\\) lists separately, including duplicates. Then\n\\[\n0\\le Z_e\\le Cmq,\\qquad \\mathbb EZ_e\\le CM/q.\n\\]</p><p>Write \\(Z_e=Z_e^{(1)}+Z_e^{(2)}\\), using target directions and source directions respectively. Group \\(Z_e^{(1)}\\) by direction \\(d\\). Distinct direction groups are pairwise independent, because\n\\[\nb_e\\longmapsto(\\pi_d(b_e),\\pi_{d'}(b_e))\n\\]\nis a bijection for \\(d\\ne d'\\). There are at most \\(Dm\\) groups. Each direction belongs to at most \\(D\\) target direction sets, and each associated target-source pair supplies at most \\(D^2\\) choices of \\((u,v)\\). Each group is bounded by \\(D^3q\\), so\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(Z_e^{(1)})\\le D^7mq^2.\n\\]</p><p>For each target \\(i\\) and direction \\(d\\), define\n\\[\nV_{i,d}=\\frac1q\\sum_{\\ell\\parallel d}\n\\left(|U_i\\cap\\ell|-\\frac{|U_i|}{q}\\right)^2.\n\\]\nThe affine second-moment identity imported from [27, Lemma A.1] is\n\\[\n\\sum_dV_{i,d}\n=|U_i|-\\frac{|U_i|^2}{q^2}\n\\le|U_i|\\le q^2.\n\\]\nIt applies to every subset of every finite affine plane, including the sets obtained after conditioning.</p><p>Each summand in \\(Z_e^{(2)}\\) is a uniformly sampled line-slice count, with variance \\(V_{i,d}\\). There are at most \\(D^3m\\) summands, and each \\((i,d)\\) occurs at most \\(D^2\\) times. Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(Z_e^{(2)})\\le D^5mA_e,\n\\qquad A_e=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\sum_{d\\in D_j}V_{i,d}.\n\\]\nEach source pool supplies at most \\(K\\) entries, and each direction belongs to at most \\(D\\) source direction sets. Thus\n\\[\n\\sum_{e\\text{ in chunk}}A_e\n\\le KD\\sum_{i\\in I}\\sum_dV_{i,d}\n\\le KDmq^2.\n\\]</p><p>Call \\(e\\) regular if \\(A_e\\le mq^{3/2}\\). There are at most \\(KD\\sqrt q\\) irregular entries. For regular \\(e\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(Z_e)\n\\le2D^7mq^2+2D^5m^2q^{3/2}.\n\\]\nFor any fixed \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\), Chebyshev’s inequality gives\n\\[\n\\Pr(Z_e&gt;CM/q+\\gamma mq)\n\\le\\frac{2D^7}{\\gamma^2m}\n+\\frac{2D^5}{\\gamma^2\\sqrt q}\n\\le B_{D,\\gamma}q^{-1/2},\n\\]\nusing \\(m&gt;c_1q\\). The constant is independent of \\(K\\).</p><p>Regularity is determined by the frozen sets and the fixed source index, not by the fresh shift. Conditional on the past, the regular \\(Z_e\\) are independent. A union bound over subsets shows that the probability of at least \\(\\gamma q\\) threshold exceedances is at most\n\\[\n2^{\\lceil\\alpha q\\rceil}\n(B_{D,\\gamma}q^{-1/2})^{\\lceil\\gamma q\\rceil}\n\\le\\exp(-c_{D,\\gamma}q\\log q).\n\\]\nIf fewer than \\(\\lceil\\gamma q\\rceil\\) regular entries exist, this event is empty.</p><p>For fixed \\(K\\), take \\(q\\) large enough that \\(KD\\sqrt q\\le\\gamma q\\). Outside the preceding exceptional event, at most \\(2\\gamma q\\) entries are irregular or exceed the threshold. The actual loss of feasible shifts is at most \\(\\sum_eZ_e\\), because counts used the larger frozen sets. Hence the end-of-chunk value satisfies\n\\[\nM'\\ge M-C\\alpha M-\\alpha\\gamma mq^2-2C\\gamma mq^2\n=\\frac12M-(\\alpha+2C)\\gamma mq^2.\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\), depending only on \\(D\\), so that\n\\[\n2(\\alpha+2C)\\gamma\\le2^{-J_0-1},\n\\qquad \\delta_1=2^{-J_0-1}.\n\\]\nInitially \\(M=mq^2\\). If all \\(J\\le J_0\\) chunks satisfy the bound, iteration gives\n\\[\n\\frac{M_{\\mathrm{fin}}}{mq^2}\n\\ge2^{-J}-(\\alpha+2C)\\gamma\\sum_{a=0}^{J-1}2^{-a}\n\\ge\\delta_1.\n\\]\nThe conditional failure bound holds for every past. Averaging and taking a union bound over the bounded number of chunks proves the feasible-density estimate. The constants do not depend on \\(K\\); only the final lower threshold on \\(q\\) does.</p><p>Condition on all shifts in \\(T\\) and suppose the density estimate succeeds. If \\(t_i\\) positions of pool \\(i\\) belong to \\(T\\), the unused positions remain independent uniform shifts. The probability that none is feasible is at most\n\\[\n\\prod_{i\\in I}\n\\left(1-\\frac{|U_i^{\\mathrm{fin}}|}{q^2}\\right)^{K-t_i}\n\\le\n\\exp\\left(-\\sum_{i\\in I}(K-t_i)\n\\frac{|U_i^{\\mathrm{fin}}|}{q^2}\\right)\n\\le e^{-(K\\delta_1-2)m}.\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\in I}|U_i^{\\mathrm{fin}}|/q^2\\ge\\delta_1m,\n\\quad 0\\le|U_i^{\\mathrm{fin}}|/q^2\\le1,\n\\quad\\sum_i t_i\\le2m.\n\\]\nThis also covers pools with no unused positions.</p><p>For \\(m&gt;c_1q\\), the number of pairs \\(I,T\\) is at most \\(A_K^m\\), where\n\\[\nA_K=\\frac{eD}{c_1}C_*K^2.\n\\]\nThe density-failure probabilities sum to\n\\[\n\\exp(O_{D,K}(q))\\exp(-\\Omega_D(q\\log q))=o(1).\n\\]\nFinally choose \\(K\\) large enough to satisfy both the earlier small-range condition and\n\\[\nA_Ke^{2-K\\delta_1}&lt;1/2.\n\\]\nThis is possible because \\(\\delta_1\\) is independent of \\(K\\). The remaining probabilities sum to at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{m&gt;c_1q}2^{-m}=o(1).\n\\]</p><p>All domination certificates are excluded with probability tending to one. Lemma 3.1 gives the independent transversal. The order of choices is\n\\[\nD;\\ C,c_1,\\alpha,J_0,\\gamma,\\delta_1;\\ K;\\ q,\n\\]\nand all estimates are uniform over the stated data.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.2, A domination certificate", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Definitions and assumptions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions and assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">A finite simple graph has a vertex partition into nonempty parts \\(A_1,\\ldots,A_n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An independent transversal is an independent set containing one vertex from each part.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(T\\subseteq S\\), say that \\(T\\) dominates \\(S\\) if every vertex of \\(S\\setminus T\\) has a neighbor in \\(T\\). No condition is imposed on vertices of \\(T\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If there is no independent transversal, then there are a nonempty index set \\(I\\) and\n\\[\nT\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i\\in I}A_i,\\qquad |T|\\le2(|I|-1),\n\\]\nsuch that \\(T\\) dominates \\(\\bigcup_{i\\in I}A_i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose an inclusion-minimal nonempty subfamily of parts with no independent transversal, and distinguish one part \\(A_*\\). Let \\(\\mathcal U\\) be the family of independent transversals of the other parts. Minimality makes \\(\\mathcal U\\ne\\varnothing\\), and the deficient subfamily has at least two parts.</p><p>Choose \\(x_1\\in A_*\\). Among \\(U\\in\\mathcal U\\), minimize \\(|N(x_1)\\cap U|\\), and fix\n\\[\nY_1=N(x_1)\\cap U.\n\\]\nThis is nonempty, since otherwise adjoining \\(x_1\\) gives a transversal.</p><p>Inductively suppose \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_j\\) and \\(Y_1,\\ldots,Y_j\\) satisfy:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The \\(Y_a\\) are nonempty and pairwise disjoint, and some \\(U\\in\\mathcal U\\) jointly realizes\n\\[\nN(x_a)\\cap U=Y_a\\quad(1\\le a\\le j).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. At stage \\(a\\), \\(|Y_a|\\) was minimized over \\(U\\in\\mathcal U\\) satisfying all earlier fixed equalities.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Each \\(x_a\\) lies in \\(A_*\\) or a part represented by an earlier \\(Y_b\\), \\(b&lt;a\\).</p><p>Let \\(Y=\\bigcup_{a=1}^jY_a\\), let \\(I\\) consist of \\(A_*\\) and the parts represented in \\(Y\\), and put\n\\[\nT=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_j\\}\\cup Y.\n\\]\nJoint realization implies that vertices of \\(Y\\) occupy distinct parts, none equal to \\(A_*\\). Hence\n\\[\n|I|=|Y|+1,\\qquad j\\le|Y|,\\qquad\n|T|\\le2|Y|=2(|I|-1).\n\\]\nThe third invariant puts \\(T\\) in the union of these parts. If \\(T\\) dominates that union, the proof is complete.</p><p>Otherwise choose \\(x_{j+1}\\) in that union, outside \\(T\\), with no neighbor in \\(T\\). Among transversals realizing all current equalities, minimize\n\\[\n|N(x_{j+1})\\cap U|,\n\\]\nand set \\(Y_{j+1}=N(x_{j+1})\\cap U\\). It avoids the earlier \\(Y_a\\).</p><p>If \\(Y_{j+1}\\) were empty and \\(x_{j+1}\\in A_*\\), adjoining \\(x_{j+1}\\) would give a transversal. Otherwise its part contains a unique \\(y\\in Y_b\\). Replace \\(y\\) in \\(U\\) by \\(x_{j+1}\\):\n\\[\nU'=(U\\setminus\\{y\\})\\cup\\{x_{j+1}\\}.\n\\]\nThis is an independent transversal of the nondistinguished parts. For \\(a&lt;b\\), the earlier equalities remain, because \\(y\\notin Y_a\\) and \\(x_{j+1}\\) is not adjacent to \\(x_a\\). For \\(a=b\\),\n\\[\n|N(x_b)\\cap U'|=|Y_b|-1,\n\\]\ncontradicting the minimum imposed at stage \\(b\\). Only equalities preceding \\(b\\) are needed for this contradiction.</p><p>Thus \\(Y_{j+1}\\ne\\varnothing\\), and the invariants continue. Each continuation adds vertices in previously unrepresented parts to \\(Y\\), while \\(Y\\) lies in a transversal of the fixed nondistinguished subfamily. There are only finitely many continuations, so the process stops with the required certificate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.\nLet \\(s\\) be a sufficiently large prime satisfying \\(s\\equiv2\\pmod3\\), and define\n\\[\nf:\\mathbb F_s^2\\to\\mathbb F_s^2,\\qquad f(x,y)=(x^3,y).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are affine lines \\(\\ell_d\\) of slope \\(d\\), one for every \\(d\\in\\mathbb F_s^*\\), such that\n\\[\nY=\\mathbb F_s^2\\setminus\\bigcup_{d\\in\\mathbb F_s^*}\\ell_d\n\\]\nhas the following properties.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. For every \\(d\\in\\mathbb F_s^*\\), every line of slope \\(d\\) other than \\(\\ell_d\\) contains at least five points of \\(Y\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The map \\(f\\) is a bijection, preserves the horizontal and vertical parallel classes, and every affine line contains at least \\(s/8\\) points of \\(f(Y)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(a\\) affine lines and \\(b\\) individual points cover \\(f(Y)\\), and\n\\[\na+b/2\\le s,\n\\]\nthen \\(a=s\\), \\(b=0\\), and the lines form a full parallel class.</p><p>Consequently every mixed line-and-point cover has weighted cost at least \\(s\\), and no cover of \\(f(Y)\\) by at most \\(s\\) lines contains two nonparallel lines.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(s\\to\\infty\\) through primes congruent to \\(2\\pmod3\\). Since \\(\\gcd(3,s-1)=1\\), cubing permutes \\(\\mathbb F_s^*\\) and fixes zero. Thus \\(f\\) is bijective and maps horizontal and vertical lines to lines in the same respective parallel classes.</p><p>Choose \\(\\ell_d\\) independently and uniformly within its parallel class. Fix a line \\(\\ell\\) of finite nonzero slope \\(d\\), and temporarily ignore \\(\\ell_d\\). Each of the other \\(s-2\\) choices deletes a uniformly distributed point of \\(\\ell\\). If \\(Z_\\ell\\) counts survivors, then\n\\[\n\\mu=\\mathbb EZ_\\ell=s(1-1/s)^{s-2}.\n\\]\nChanging one choice changes the count by at most two. The independent-trial exposure and bounded-drift estimates from [27, Lemmas A.2–A.3], with \\(T=s-2\\), \\(L=2\\), and deviation \\(\\mu/2\\), give\n\\[\n\\Pr(Z_\\ell&lt;\\mu/2)\n\\le2\\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\mu^2}{128(s-2)}\\right)\n=\\exp(-\\Omega(s)).\n\\]\nThere are fewer than \\(s^2\\) such lines. A union bound and \\(\\mu/2\\ge5\\) for sufficiently large \\(s\\) give the first property with probability tending to one. If \\(\\ell\\ne\\ell_d\\), reinstating \\(\\ell_d\\) changes no point of \\(\\ell\\).</p><p>In the new coordinates, possible deletion shapes are\n\\[\nF_{d,z}=\\{(x^3,dx+z):x\\in\\mathbb F_s\\},\n\\qquad d\\in\\mathbb F_s^*,\\ z\\in\\mathbb F_s.\n\\]\nThere are \\(s(s-1)\\) shapes of size \\(s\\), and each of \\(s-1\\) independent trials chooses one. Each point is deleted by a specified trial with probability \\(1/s\\), giving uniform survival probability\n\\[\n\\delta_s=(1-1/s)^{s-1}\\ge1/4\n\\]\nfor large \\(s\\).</p><p>Each shape meets every affine line in at most three points. For vertical lines, use bijectivity of cubing; for horizontal lines, use \\(d\\ne0\\). For finite nonzero slope \\(m\\), intersections satisfy\n\\[\ndx+z=mx^3+u,\n\\]\na nonzero polynomial equation of degree three. In particular no shape is a line for \\(s&gt;5\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition 2 with field size \\(s\\), \\(D=3\\), survival lower bound \\(1/4\\), and \\(\\rho_0=1/100\\). Exact-line occurrence probabilities are zero, and the subfield restriction is automatic. With probability tending to one, every line contains at least \\(s/8\\) points of \\(f(Y)\\), and every line set of size at most \\(s\\) covering all but at most \\(2s\\) points contains at least \\((1-\\rho_0)s\\) lines through a common projective point.</p><p>Assume these events and the original-coordinate event hold. Consider a cover with \\(a+b/2\\le s\\). Repeated resources may be removed. Its line portion has at most \\(s\\) distinct lines and leaves at most \\(b\\le2s\\) points uncovered. It therefore contains at least \\((1-\\rho_0)s\\) lines through a projective point \\(p\\). There are at most \\(\\rho_0s\\) other lines, and\n\\[\nb\\le2(s-a)\\le2\\rho_0s.\n\\]</p><p>If an affine line through \\(p\\) were missing, the chosen pencil lines would cover at most one affine point of it, the other lines at most \\(\\rho_0s\\), and individual points at most \\(2\\rho_0s\\). This contradicts the line lower bound, because\n\\[\ns/8&gt;1+3\\rho_0s\n\\]\nfor large \\(s\\). Every affine line through \\(p\\) is therefore present. A finite center requires \\(s+1\\) lines, impossible. An infinite center requires all \\(s\\) lines of its parallel class, and the budget forces \\(a=s,b=0\\). This also rules out repetitions at equality.</p><p>All events hold simultaneously with probability tending to one, proving existence for every sufficiently large eligible prime. A mixed cover of cost less than \\(s\\) would satisfy the budget but fail its necessary equality conclusion. A line cover with at most \\(s\\) lines must be a full parallel class.</p><p>There are arbitrarily large primes congruent to \\(2\\pmod3\\): if their finite product were \\(P\\), then \\(3P-1\\equiv2\\pmod3\\) would have a prime factor congruent to \\(2\\pmod3\\), and none of the primes dividing \\(P\\) could divide it.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "This proposition collects the ambient-placement construction of Section 6.", "statement": "<p>This proposition collects the ambient-placement construction of Section 6. Its lemmas retain the original labels Lemma 6.1 and Lemma 6.2.</p><p>Fixed small-field data.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a sufficiently large prime \\(s\\equiv2\\pmod3\\), together with \\(Y\\), its omitted lines, and \\(f(x,y)=(x^3,y)\\) from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\ge3\\) be odd, put \\(q=s^n\\), and identify \\(\\mathbb F_s\\) with the prime subfield of \\(\\mathbb F_q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Identify ambient directions with \\(\\mathbb P^1(\\mathbb F_q)=\\mathbb F_q\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\), using slope.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose representatives \\(\\Lambda\\) for \\(\\mathbb F_q^*/\\mathbb F_s^*\\), with \\(1\\in\\Lambda\\), and put\n\\[\nI=\\Lambda\\sqcup\\{*\\},\\qquad\nN_q=|I|=\\frac{q-1}{s-1}+1.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\lambda\\in\\Lambda\\), define\n\\[\nM_\\lambda(x,y)=(x,\\lambda y),\\qquad\nS_\\lambda=\\lambda\\mathbb F_s^*,\\qquad\nA_\\lambda=\\{0,\\infty\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose distinct \\(u,v\\in\\mathbb F_s^*\\), and define\n\\[\nM_*=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\u&amp;v\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nS_*=(\\mathbb F_s\\cup\\{\\infty\\})\\setminus\\{u,v\\},\n\\qquad A_*=\\{u,v\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nW_i=M_i(\\mathbb F_s^2),\\qquad\nD_i=\n\\begin{cases}\nS_i\\cup A_i,&amp;i\\in\\{1,*\\},\\\\\nS_i,&amp;i\\notin\\{1,*\\}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every proper subfield of \\(\\mathbb F_q\\) has order at most \\(q^{1/3}\\). The join directions of distinct points of \\(W_i\\) are exactly \\(S_i\\cup A_i\\). Every ambient direction belongs to at least one and at most two \\(S_i\\); the double memberships are precisely\n\\[\nS_1\\cap S_*=\\mathbb F_s^*\\setminus\\{u,v\\}.\n\\]\nFurthermore,\n\\[\nD_1=D_*=\\mathbb P^1(\\mathbb F_s),\n\\]\nand every direction belongs to at most two \\(D_i\\).</p><p>There are constants \\(K\\), \\(\\delta\\in(0,1)\\), and \\(\\rho=\\delta/10\\), independent of \\(n\\), such that for every sufficiently large odd \\(n\\) there exist shifts \\(b_i\\in\\mathbb F_q^2\\) with the following properties. Set\n\\[\nP_i=b_i+W_i,\\qquad Y_i=b_i+M_iY,\n\\]\n\\[\nB_i=\\{\\ell:\\operatorname{dir}(\\ell)\\in S_i,\\ \\ell\\cap Y_i\\ne\\varnothing\\},\n\\qquad B=\\bigcup_{i\\in I}B_i,\n\\]\n\\[\nX=\\mathbb F_q^2\\setminus\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in B}\\ell.\n\\]\nThen:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The \\(P_i\\) are pairwise disjoint, and\n\\[\ni\\ne j,\\quad \\operatorname{dir}(\\ell)\\in D_i,\\quad\n\\ell\\cap P_i\\ne\\varnothing\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\ell\\cap P_j=\\varnothing.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For every \\(i\\) and \\(d\\in S_i\\), exactly \\(s-1\\) lines of \\(B_i\\) have direction \\(d\\), and every such line contains at least five points of \\(Y_i\\). Thus \\(|B_i|=(s-1)^2\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Every cover of \\(X\\) by at most \\(q\\) affine lines contains at least \\((1-\\rho)q\\) lines through a common projective point.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Every \\(\\ell\\notin B\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|X\\cap\\ell|\\ge\\delta q/2.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">5. For every finite point \\(p\\) and affine line \\(\\ell\\) not through \\(p\\), at most five groups \\(i\\) contain a point \\(y\\in P_i\\cap\\ell\\) whose join to \\(p\\) has direction in \\(S_i\\).</p><p>The shifts may depend on \\(n\\), while all constants are fixed after \\(s,Y\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(E\\) is a subfield of \\(\\mathbb F_q\\), then \\(|E|=s^a\\), with\n\\[\nn=a[\\mathbb F_q:E].\n\\]\nFor proper \\(E\\), the index is an odd integer larger than one, hence at least three. Thus\n\\[\n|E|\\le s^{n/3}=q^{1/3}.\n\\]</p><p>The maps \\(M_\\lambda\\) are invertible and induce the slope maps \\(t\\mapsto\\lambda t\\). The map \\(M_*\\) is invertible because \\(u\\ne v\\), and induces\n\\[\nt\\longmapsto\\frac{u+vt}{1+t},\n\\]\nwith zero denominator interpreted as infinity. Its images of the horizontal and vertical directions are \\(u,v\\); its images of the finite nonzero subfield slopes are exactly \\(S_*\\).</p><p>Consequently the join directions in \\(W_i\\) are exactly \\(S_i\\cup A_i\\). The sets \\(S_\\lambda\\) partition all finite nonzero ambient slopes. The extra \\(S_*\\) contains \\(0,\\infty\\), and its nonzero slopes lie in \\(S_1\\). Hence every direction belongs to one or two \\(S_i\\), with double memberships precisely\n\\[\nS_1\\cap S_*=\\mathbb F_s^*\\setminus\\{u,v\\}.\n\\]\nThe stated \\(D_i\\) satisfy\n\\[\nD_1=D_*=\\mathbb P^1(\\mathbb F_s),\n\\]\nwhile the other \\(D_i\\) are nonidentity multiplicative cosets. Each direction occurs at most twice.</p><p>Since \\(|W_i|=s^2\\), \\(|D_i|\\le s+1\\), and \\(N_q=O_s(q)\\), a fixed constant depending only on \\(s\\) satisfies the hypotheses of Proposition 3. Fix its pool size \\(K\\), and sample the pools of Lemma 5.2.</p><p>With probability tending to one, Proposition 3 supplies a conflict-free choice of one candidate from each group. Intersect this event with the events of Lemma 5.2. No independence between these events is needed. For sufficiently large odd \\(n\\), their intersection has positive probability. Fix a pool and a selection in that intersection.</p><p>Write its selected shifts and sets as \\(b_i,P_i,Y_i\\). Conflict avoidance gives\n\\[\ni\\ne j,\\quad \\operatorname{dir}(\\ell)\\in D_i,\\quad\n\\ell\\cap P_i\\ne\\varnothing\n\\Longrightarrow\\ell\\cap P_j=\\varnothing.\n\\]\nThe \\(P_i\\) are pairwise disjoint, since a common point would give zero difference in every conflict direction, and every \\(D_i\\) is nonempty. The selected line counts and occupancy follow from Lemma 5.1.</p><p>Only selected candidates are deleted, so\n\\[\nX\\supseteq X_{\\mathrm{pool}}.\n\\]\nAny cover of \\(X\\) covers \\(X_{\\mathrm{pool}}\\), proving the almost-pencil property.</p><p>If \\(\\ell\\notin B\\), every selected deletion line still occurs in the pool process with exact occurrences of \\(\\ell\\) omitted. The survivor set of that modified pool process is therefore contained in \\(X\\). This is deterministic, even if \\(\\ell\\) occurs in an unselected candidate or the selection depends on the entire pool. Lemma 5.2 yields\n\\[\n|X\\cap\\ell|\\ge\\delta q/2.\n\\]\nThe five-group bound also transfers, because it was established for every candidate in the pool.</p><p>The order of choices is \\(s,Y\\), then the selection bound and \\(K\\), then the deletion constants \\(D,\\delta,\\rho\\), and finally \\(q=s^n\\). The ambient deletion constant may depend on \\(K\\), but changes no hypothesis of the already applied selection proposition. A small \\(\\delta\\) only increases the final threshold on \\(q\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.\nUse the fixed small-field data and ambient maps of Proposition 5. For any group \\(i\\) and any shift \\(b\\in\\mathbb F_q^2\\), put\n\\[\nP=b+M_i(\\mathbb F_s^2),\\qquad Y'=b+M_iY,\n\\]\n\\[\nB'=\\{\\ell:\\operatorname{dir}(\\ell)\\in S_i,\\ \\ell\\cap Y'\\ne\\varnothing\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(d\\in S_i\\), exactly \\(s-1\\) lines of \\(B'\\) have direction \\(d\\), each containing at least five points of \\(Y'\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n|B'|=(s-1)^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Undo the translation and apply \\(M_i^{-1}\\). An ambient line in the given direction meeting \\(P\\) becomes a line through a point of \\(\\mathbb F_s^2\\) in a finite nonzero subfield direction. Its intersection with \\(\\mathbb F_s^2\\) is the corresponding subfield line.</p><p>The \\(s\\) lines of that subfield parallel class extend to distinct parallel ambient lines. Exactly one is the omitted line in the definition of \\(Y\\); the other \\(s-1\\) each contain at least five points of \\(Y\\), by Proposition 4. These are precisely the lines meeting \\(Y'\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed small-field data and ambient maps of Proposition 5, with \\(q=s^n\\) and odd \\(n\\to\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\) be a fixed pool size supplied by Proposition 3 for these configuration and direction bounds.</p><p class=\"li\">Independently for all \\(i\\in I\\) and \\(1\\le k\\le K\\), choose uniform shifts \\(b_{i,k}\\in\\mathbb F_q^2\\), and put\n\\[\nP_{i,k}=b_{i,k}+W_i,\\qquad\nY_{i,k}=b_{i,k}+M_iY,\n\\]\n\\[\nB_{i,k}=\n\\{\\ell:\\operatorname{dir}(\\ell)\\in S_i,\\ \\ell\\cap Y_{i,k}\\ne\\varnothing\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nX_{\\mathrm{pool}}\n=\\mathbb F_q^2\\setminus\n\\bigcup_{i\\in I}\\bigcup_{k=1}^K\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in B_{i,k}}\\ell.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are constants \\(\\delta\\in(0,1)\\) and \\(\\rho=\\delta/10\\), independent of \\(n\\), such that with probability tending to one:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every cover of \\(X_{\\mathrm{pool}}\\) by at most \\(q\\) affine lines contains at least \\((1-\\rho)q\\) lines through a common projective point.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For every affine line \\(\\ell\\), ignoring only exact occurrences of \\(\\ell\\) in the pool deletions leaves at least \\(\\delta q/2\\) points of \\(\\ell\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For every finite \\(p\\) and affine line \\(\\ell\\not\\ni p\\), at most five groups \\(i\\) have a candidate \\(P_{i,k}\\) containing a point \\(y\\in\\ell\\) whose join to \\(p\\) has direction in \\(S_i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Treat each candidate shift as an independent deletion trial, listing \\(B_{i,k}\\). By Lemma 5.1, there are \\(KN_q=O_{s,K}(q)\\) trials, each listing \\(m=(s-1)^2\\) lines. Take all \\(q(q+1)\\) affine lines as the shape family. Each has \\(q\\) points, and distinct line shapes meet in at most one point. The shape and trial bounds of Proposition 2 hold for a sufficiently large fixed integer \\(D\\), depending only on \\(s,K\\).</p><p>A candidate deletion set is a uniform translate of a fixed union of \\(m\\) lines. Its deletion probability at a fixed point is independent of the point and at most \\(m/q\\). Independence makes the overall survival probability \\(\\delta_q\\) uniform. For large \\(q\\), \\(m/q\\le1/2\\) and \\(N_q\\le2q/(s-1)\\). Since \\(\\log(1-t)\\ge-2t\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le1/2\\),\n\\[\n\\delta_q\\ge(1-m/q)^{KN_q}\n\\ge\\exp(-2KN_qm/q)\n\\ge\\exp(-4K(s-1)).\n\\]\nFix\n\\[\n\\delta=\\exp(-4K(s-1)),\\qquad \\rho=\\delta/10.\n\\]</p><p>A fixed line \\(\\ell\\) of direction \\(d\\) can occur only in groups with \\(d\\in S_i\\), of which there are at most two. Each candidate in such a group has \\(s-1\\) translated lines of this direction, each becoming \\(\\ell\\) with probability \\(1/q\\). The sum of exact-line occurrence probabilities is therefore at most\n\\[\n2K(s-1)/q.\n\\]\nIncreasing \\(D\\) verifies all hypotheses of Proposition 2, which gives the first two assertions.</p><p>For the third, fix \\(p\\notin\\ell\\). For one candidate in group \\(i\\), there are at most \\(s-1\\) possible points \\(y\\in\\ell\\) whose join to \\(p\\) has direction in \\(S_i\\). A fixed point belongs to a uniform translate of \\(W_i\\) with probability \\(s^2/q^2\\), so the candidate probability is at most\n\\[\n(s-1)s^2/q^2.\n\\]\nIf six distinct groups qualify, choose one witnessing candidate from each. There are at most\n\\[\n(KN_q)^6=O_{s,K}(q^6)\n\\]\nchoices, with independent shifts. For the fixed pair \\((p,\\ell)\\), the probability is \\(O_{s,K}(q^{-6})\\). There are at most\n\\[\nq^2q(q+1)=O(q^4)\n\\]\npairs. A union bound gives total failure probability \\(O_{s,K}(q^{-2})\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 7.1, together with the hypergraph definition in Section 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.\nUse \\(s,q,Y,f,I,M_i,S_i,A_i,P_i,Y_i,B,X\\) from Proposition 5, with \\(q\\) sufficiently large.</p><p>For each ambient direction \\(d\\in\\mathbb P^1(\\mathbb F_q)\\), create a vertex part \\(V_d\\):</p><p class=\"li\">For every ambient affine line \\(\\ell\\) of direction \\(d\\), put an ordinary vertex \\(o_\\ell\\) in \\(V_d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(d\\in S_i\\), let \\(t_i(d)\\in\\mathbb F_s^*\\) be the unique direction mapped to \\(d\\) by \\(M_i\\). For every subfield affine line \\(L\\) of direction \\(t_i(d)\\), put a special vertex \\(v_{i,d,L}\\) in \\(V_d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All vertices are formal labels, distinct between parts, between ordinary and special types, and between groups.</p><p>Let \\(\\ell_d(z)\\) denote the ambient line of direction \\(d\\) through \\(z\\), and let \\(L_t(w)\\) denote the subfield line of direction \\(t\\) through \\(w\\in\\mathbb F_s^2\\).</p><p>For \\(x\\in X\\), define\n\\[\ne_x=\\{o_{\\ell_d(x)}:d\\in\\mathbb P^1(\\mathbb F_q)\\}.\n\\]\nFor \\(i\\in I,w\\in Y\\), put \\(y_{i,w}=b_i+M_iw\\), and define \\(e_{i,w}\\) by its vertex in part \\(V_d\\):\n\\[\ne_{i,w}\\cap V_d=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{v_{i,d,L_{t_i(d)}(f(w))}\\},&amp;d\\in S_i,\\\\\n\\{o_{\\ell_d(y_{i,w})}\\},&amp;d\\notin S_i.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\nH=\\{e_x:x\\in X\\}\\cup\\{e_{i,w}:i\\in I,\\ w\\in Y\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The hypergraph \\(H\\) is finite, nonempty, \\((q+1)\\)-partite, \\((q+1)\\)-uniform, and intersecting. All edges in the displayed parametrization are distinct.</p>", "proof": "<p>There are \\(q+1\\) parts, and every edge contains exactly one vertex of every part. All vertex sets are finite. The hypergraph is nonempty because \\(Y\\ne\\varnothing\\).</p><p>Two ordinary edges with distinct base points share the ordinary label of their join.</p><p>For an exceptional base point \\(y\\in Y_i\\), every direction in the nonempty set \\(S_i\\) gives a deletion line through \\(y\\), so \\(y\\notin X\\). If \\(x\\in X\\), the join of \\(x,y\\) cannot have direction in \\(S_i\\), since otherwise it would be a selected deletion line containing \\(x\\). Its ordinary label belongs to both edges.</p><p>For exceptional edges from distinct groups \\(i,j\\), the base points are distinct by Proposition 5. Compatibility puts their join direction outside both \\(D_i,D_j\\), and hence outside \\(S_i\\cup S_j\\). They share its ordinary label.</p><p>For \\(e_{i,w},e_{i,w'}\\) with \\(w\\ne w'\\), the points \\(f(w),f(w')\\) are distinct. If their join has finite nonzero slope \\(t\\), the edges share the special label of that line in direction \\(M_i(t)\\). If the join is horizontal or vertical, the corresponding original coordinates are equal as well, because \\(f\\) preserves those two parallel classes bijectively. The original base points share an ordinary label in the corresponding direction of \\(A_i\\). Thus \\(H\\) is intersecting.</p><p>Two ordinary labels in nonparallel directions determine the ordinary base point, so ordinary edges are distinct. Every exceptional edge has special labels, so cannot equal an ordinary edge. Within a group, special labels in two distinct directions of \\(S_i\\) determine \\(f(w)\\), because the corresponding subfield lines are nonparallel. Since \\(|S_i|=s-1\\ge2\\), equal exceptional edges in a group would have \\(w=w'\\). A special label tagged by group \\(i\\) occurs in no edge of another group, so edges of distinct groups are also distinct.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma 7.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Assumptions and definitions.\nUse the hypergraph of Proposition 6. Fix a group \\(i\\), and identify its exceptional edges with positions in \\(f(Y)\\) by\n\\[\ne_{i,w}\\longleftrightarrow f(w).\n\\]\nThe action of a covering vertex is the set of positions whose exceptional edges contain it.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. A special vertex of group \\(i\\) covers positions on one subfield line. A special vertex of any other group covers none.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. An ordinary vertex of direction in \\(S_i\\) covers none. An ordinary vertex of direction in \\(A_i\\), if it covers any positions, covers them on one horizontal or vertical line in the \\(f\\)-coordinates.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. An ordinary vertex of direction outside \\(S_i\\cup A_i\\) covers at most one position.</p><p>In particular, the positions covered by any one vertex are contained in a subfield affine line.</p>", "proof": "<p>The special-vertex assertions and the assertion for ordinary vertices in altered directions follow directly from the edge definition.</p><p>For an axis direction, undoing \\(b_i,M_i\\) fixes one original coordinate. Applying \\(f\\) gives the corresponding horizontal or vertical line.</p><p>A line in a direction outside \\(S_i\\cup A_i\\) cannot contain two points of \\(P_i\\), since the join directions of that embedded subplane are exactly \\(S_i\\cup A_i\\). It therefore covers at most one exceptional position. A singleton can be included in any subfield line through it, and an empty set requires no resource.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 7, "cite"], [3, 5, "cite"], [3, 6, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 2, "cite"], [7, 11, "cite"], [7, 14, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [9, 12, "cite"], [9, 14, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [11, 13, "cite"], [12, 0, "cite"], [12, 15, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 12, "cite"], [15, 0, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 0, "cite"]]});
